{"timestamp":"2026-08-11T11:12:46.888Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_019ff086-6328-7f33-ab21-a3977b63a422","author":"/root/rich_render_parity_audit","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/rich_render_parity_audit\nPayload:\nAudit result: the rich-rendering foundation is real, but it does not yet meet the plan’s “every web-accepted Mermaid grammar” or cross-ingress contract.\n\n- P0: Mermaid parity is falsely bounded to 12 grammars. [`rendering.ts`](packages/learner-contracts/src/rendering.ts:40) declares only 12, and mobile rejects everything else. But web calls Mermaid directly with no matching grammar allowlist ([`MermaidRenderer.tsx`](web/src/components/MermaidRenderer.tsx:192)), so its installed Mermaid accepts additional grammars. I verified `architecture`, `sankey`, `xychart`, `requirement`, and `block` via `mermaid.detectType()`. Thus the fixture pack and mobile are not covering “every grammar the current web renderer accepts.”\n\n- P1: OpenUI fence aliases diverge. Mobile accepts `keating-ui`, `ui-document`, `openui-json`, and `openui` ([`ui-document-wire.ts`](mobile/src/lib/ui-document-wire.ts:14)); web accepts only `openui` / `openui-lang` ([`segments.ts`](web/src/keating/openui/segments.ts:9)). A persisted ` ```keating-ui ` JSON document is rendered natively on mobile but leaks as ordinary Markdown/source on web/desktop. Canonical JSON inside ` ```openui ` and browser source both work, but aliases are not cross-surface.\n\n- P1: Web Mermaid theme switching is stale. Theme is read during render ([`MermaidRenderer.tsx`](web/src/components/MermaidRenderer.tsx:196-209)), but the effect only depends on `content` ([`MermaidRenderer.tsx`](web/src/components/MermaidRenderer.tsx:170)); switching light/dark without changing the message keeps the prior SVG. The 200-character cache key ([`MermaidRenderer.tsx`](web/src/components/MermaidRenderer.tsx:181-189)) can also return the wrong diagram for same-prefix inputs.\n\n- P2: “Streaming fences do not leak” has strong parser/unit coverage, including partial source and atomic statements, but no rendered browser streaming run that observes chunk-by-chunk content. The focused tests prove parser behavior, not an actual provider/renderer stream.\n\n- Desktop does inherit the actual web renderer: packaged Electron starts local Nitro and loads `/chat` ([`main.ts`](desktop/src/main.ts:106-119)), rather than a separate desktop Markdown/OpenUI implementation. This is the right architecture. Current evidence is packaged smoke/reporting, not a maintained desktop-specific rich-rendering acceptance test.\n\n- TUI is honest, not graphical parity: canonical JSON is interactive, Markdown uses OpenTUI, Mermaid is source plus handoff. Browser OpenUI source is deliberately not compiled by the terminal adapter ([`adapter.ts`](src/tui/ui/adapter.ts:154-169)), which is acceptable only under the plan’s explicit “preserved for handoff” clause.\n\nWhat is already solid:\n- Shared fixture v2, safe source compiler, JSON/source canonical convergence, atomic actions, mobile source hiding, and TUI terminal semantics all have focused deterministic tests.\n- I ran the focused matrix: 110 tests, 835 assertions, zero failures.\n\nSmallest next implementation tranche:\n\n1. Make one shared OpenUI wire parser/normalizer used by web and mobile; support the four declared JSON fence labels everywhere, while retaining browser-source only for `openui`.\n2. Define the actual supported Mermaid policy once:\n   - either restrict web to the same explicit allowlist, or\n   - expand the contract/fixtures/mobile restricted renderer to every Mermaid grammar intentionally supported by the shipped web version.\n   The former is much smaller and safer.\n3. Key Mermaid cache by full source plus resolved theme, and subscribe/rerender on theme change.\n4. Add rendered acceptance, not just parser tests:\n   - Web/desktop: streamed partial fence → no source visible → closure → one document.\n   - Web/desktop: aliases and each declared Mermaid grammar.\n   - Mobile device: same ingress matrix and light/dark diagram switch.\n   - TUI: assert source-program handoff wording for an `openui` fence.\n\nRecommended exact checks:\n\n```bash\nrtk bun test ./packages/learner-contracts/test ./web/src/test/rendering-parity-contract.test.ts ./web/src/test/openui-segments.test.ts ./mobile/test/mermaid-native.test.ts ./mobile/test/ui-document-wire.test.ts ./test/tui-terminal-presentation.test.ts ./test/tui-ui.test.ts\nrtk devenv tasks run keating:test-web\nrtk devenv tasks run keating:mobile-check\nrtk devenv tasks run keating:desktop-build\nrtk devenv tasks run keating:desktop-package-smoke\nrtk devenv tasks run keating:test\n```\n\nDocumentation is internally stale/inconsistent: execution says 316/1,299 mobile tests, baseline says 315/1,296; neither substitute for physical Android acceptance. The plan itself correctly says that device/runtime evidence remains required.\n\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"93ef4b9d-3191-46b6-a2c2-3b39e130995b"}}}
